So, in order for me to be a learner in the next few minutes here, I

00:02:12.105 --> 00:02:16.898
need to enlist the help of this giant blue foam finger to ask you the first

00:02:16.898 --> 00:02:23.799
question. So, Kate, my, I'm dying to know, What is the number one thing that marketers

00:02:23.913 --> 00:02:26.564
need to do in order to connect to their market?

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:32.343
Kate Wood: Yes, I think every person is gonna have a different opinion on this one, um, but

00:02:32.343 --> 00:02:36.327
I think the number one thing marketers really need to do is just follow the emotion.

00:02:36.842 --> 00:02:42.776
Um, we live in the B2B space, we're often talking about highly complex products, technology, infrastructure,

00:02:43.391 --> 00:02:46.209
um, and we're oftentimes selling into highly sophisticated markets.

00:02:46.886 --> 00:02:52.400
However, we're still human beings and we still make decisions from our limbic system, the amygdala,

00:02:52.416 --> 00:02:56.441
our emotional center. We actually don't make decisions from our prefrontal cortex.

00:02:56.506 --> 00:03:00.804
We do a lot of data and analysis from there, but you've probably heard the phrase,

00:03:01.141 --> 00:03:03.788
people don't buy from companies, they buy from people they like.

00:03:04.205 --> 00:03:09.353
And so your job as a marketer is to follow the emotion, map out your sales

00:03:09.353 --> 00:03:13.828
cycle, map out the stages within each of that within that sales cycle and kind of

00:03:13.828 --> 00:03:20.289
understand, okay, What emotional experience, whether it's through materials, through an event, through a series of

00:03:20.321 --> 00:03:25.419
meetings, what emotional experience are we creating for our prospects and our clients at each stage?

00:03:25.852 --> 00:03:29.170
What are they feeling? What do they love about us and what do they hate about

00:03:29.234 --> 00:03:35.357
us? Map that out and kind of understand how you can uh double down on those

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:41.321
emotional experiences that are really creating positive engagement and connectivity between your sales team, your customer

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.821
success team, your products and your prospects and your clients.

00:03:44.431 --> 00:03:47.835
And then where are you, where's their lackluster, where do Where do people ghost you?

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.522
Where do pe- where does the kind of the sales, what sales stage goes dead a

00:03:52.522 --> 00:03:57.295
little bit? And then how can you as a marketer bring that creativity, that inspiration, uh

00:03:57.522 --> 00:04:01.571
that humanity to reengage the prospect and get them more involved with you?

00:04:01.895 --> 00:04:03.709
So again, kind of follow that emotion.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.049
Casey Cheshire: Oh man, follow the emotion.

00:04:07.804 --> 00:04:09.109
You are just so on brand too.

00:04:09.237 --> 00:04:13.779
This is fantastic. And I and I'm I'm dying to dig into that.

00:04:13.843 --> 00:04:16.597
So I'm glad that that's part of your answer or is the answer.

00:04:17.546 --> 00:04:21.296
But before you even get into the the the hows and the whats, why do you

00:04:21.328 --> 00:04:25.495
think we're not doing this generally in marketing, or not enough of us are?

00:04:26.537 --> 00:04:30.367
Kate Wood: Well, I think your career arc, and not to get too much on a soapbox, but

00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:33.876
I think your career arc kind of follows a certain trajectory.

00:04:33.988 --> 00:04:38.860
When you're younger, you're in rooms, there are big words being thrown around, there are big

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.665
powerful people, and you're younger and you're just trying to learn, right?

00:04:41.825 --> 00:04:45.094
And you're just trying to mimic and mirror, and you're learning through modeling.

00:04:45.206 --> 00:04:49.982
And so oftentimes we find ourselves at a certain stage in our career where we're using

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:56.202
a more sophisticated lexicon, we're using run-on sentences and ad copy, um we're kind of talking

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:59.247
inside baseball to prove that we know what we know.

00:05:00.177 --> 00:05:04.714
And my hope and my own personal experience is kind of once you get over that

00:05:04.906 --> 00:05:08.753
ego hump of proving that you're valuable and you're worth it, and really once you start

00:05:08.753 --> 00:05:12.969
to settle in and you understand your own value and your own worth as a marketer,

00:05:13.258 --> 00:05:18.083
that's when the real magic can start to happen because you kind of lose that ego

00:05:18.227 --> 00:05:23.454
and you lose that lexicon for proving yourself and you stay more focused on, okay, from

00:05:23.519 --> 00:05:26.069
a performance standpoint, what is working for us?

00:05:26.486 --> 00:05:29.260
And then qualitatively, why is it working for us?

00:05:29.773 --> 00:05:37.103
And how do we bring language, emotion, experience into each touchpoint throughout the sales cycle to

00:05:38.273 --> 00:05:41.128
accelerate deal velocity, to increase average deal size?

00:05:41.625 --> 00:05:46.180
Um, it is that blend between the quantitative and the qualitative, but it is speaking in

00:05:46.196 --> 00:05:49.869
a way to your market that humanizes whatever it is that you're selling.

00:05:50.407 --> 00:05:54.426
Um, again, people don't buy things, they buy from people they like.

00:05:54.571 --> 00:05:59.893
They buy relationships and experiences that make them feel a certain way about the kind of

00:05:59.927 --> 00:06:01.170
professional that they are.

00:06:03.273 --> 00:06:08.189
Casey Cheshire: Yeah, and you've definitely mentioned humanity like twice now, and I'm like, I want to click

00:06:08.254 --> 00:06:08.743
into that.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.202
How, how are we going against that?

00:06:12.267 --> 00:06:16.015
How are we sort of losing the humanity of the prospect on the other end or

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.874
the the customer on the other side?

00:06:18.549 --> 00:06:27.811
Kate Wood: Yeah, I mean, the code word for anything anti-humanity is kind of scale, distribution, uh automation,

00:06:27.923 --> 00:06:30.202
velocity. These are all terms we throw around.

00:06:30.347 --> 00:06:33.926
They actually mean things. They mean very important things, and they are actually very important for

00:06:33.958 --> 00:06:37.137
business growth. So, they are not bad words.

00:06:37.811 --> 00:06:42.675
Your challenge as a marketer is to understand, okay, I have to hit certain metrics.

00:06:42.771 --> 00:06:48.324
I have to hit whether it's revenue targets, whether it's margin, um I have these targets

00:06:48.324 --> 00:06:53.982
that I have to hit, but I need to be able to find the emotion in

00:06:54.094 --> 00:07:00.346
my sales process such that I can continue to either create more of that emotion to

00:07:00.346 --> 00:07:05.332
be able to create more prospects, to create more closed won deals, or I need to

00:07:05.476 --> 00:07:10.750
look at where we're really failing our clients and our prospects from an emotional standpoint and

00:07:10.766 --> 00:07:14.276
how as a marketer, what is in my purview to be able to fix that?

00:07:14.613 --> 00:07:19.743
Is it conversations in different processes with product, with service delivery, with the customer success team.

00:07:20.208 --> 00:07:21.988
Um, what does that look like?

00:07:22.132 --> 00:07:24.971
And how do I- again, you're a marketer, right?

00:07:25.035 --> 00:07:29.381
Like, you're not everything to everybody, but you do have skilled expertise.

00:07:29.589 --> 00:07:33.582
And marketing, if you think about it from a social science standpoint, as well as branding,

00:07:33.999 --> 00:07:37.142
it's your psychology, it's your sociology, it's your anthropology.

00:07:37.447 --> 00:07:42.579
That is all a human, whether it's a human individual, whether it's humans together, or whether

00:07:42.611 --> 00:07:45.321
it's the culture that humans cultivate within one another.

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:49.814
All of that is about that emotional connectivity and what lights you up and what doesn't.

00:07:50.199 --> 00:07:56.153
Um and so I think um to kind of bring it back around, that's why I

00:07:56.169 --> 00:08:00.036
think humanity is so important when you're thinking about, okay, we do have to go to

00:08:00.068 --> 00:08:07.883
market at scale, yes, but where can we find the sparkly emotional points that will help

00:08:07.883 --> 00:08:13.131
us reverse engineer something that will actually create more revenue in the long run?

00:08:13.548 --> 00:08:15.185
But that starts at the emotional level.

00:08:15.249 --> 00:08:18.138
It doesn't start It doesn't start again in the prefrontal cortex.

00:08:19.503 --> 00:08:21.141
Casey Cheshire: But it's a balance. That's interesting.

00:08:21.414 --> 00:08:25.846
I I I think too often it's the folks that say like, oh, email is dead

00:08:25.846 --> 00:08:31.594
or this is dead. And um, but no, we're not saying, we're not saying we're anti-scale.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:32.686
You need scale.

00:08:33.954 --> 00:08:40.618
But we're saying that sometimes these words run against the idea of understanding more about the

00:08:40.618 --> 00:08:43.360
individual. And you need to balance it.

00:08:43.888 --> 00:08:46.788
How, how is it, is it a balance that can change over time?

00:08:46.852 --> 00:08:52.188
Like, can we start out like leaning heavy into humanity and then as we understand more

00:08:52.236 --> 00:08:55.872
and more about the people, we can pull back a little bit and scale more?

00:08:56.641 --> 00:08:59.814
Kate Wood: Yeah, and I think too, just to like, let's bring this down a little bit, 'cause

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:03.130
it, it's easy for me to sit here and say, oh, it's all about the humanity.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.934
And then if I'm a younger marketer, I'm like, what the hell does she mean by

00:09:05.998 --> 00:09:10.148
that? Like, that's a philosophy. Like, talk to me about tactics and tangible, like, how do

00:09:10.164 --> 00:09:14.930
I actually make that um kind of philosophy around humanity, bring it to life from a

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:19.667
marketing standpoint. And what it really means is having processes and conversations in place with your

00:09:19.667 --> 00:09:23.779
sales team and your customer success team, and probably most likely your product team as well.

00:09:24.453 --> 00:09:29.415
Um, because what you want to understand is you're always gonna have champions and you're always

00:09:29.431 --> 00:09:34.008
gonna have haters. And if you can make friends with both, you can understand so much

00:09:34.105 --> 00:09:37.830
about what your product or your service does well, why.

00:09:38.926 --> 00:09:43.113
And then how that gives you all you need to know as a roadmap, as a

00:09:43.113 --> 00:09:47.127
marketer, to be able to then go double down, reinvest, or again, kind of walk away

00:09:47.127 --> 00:09:49.199
from a certain channel or a certain tactic.

00:09:49.664 --> 00:09:56.472
Um, and so I think, um, I I think that's where we can bring that humanity

00:09:56.504 --> 00:10:01.466
to life, um, is to just kind of really understand your champions and your haters and

00:10:01.643 --> 00:10:01.883
and why.

00:10:05.181 --> 00:10:08.636
Casey Cheshire: Yeah, and like, I guess I've never thought about the haters group.

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:10.031
What do you do with that information?

00:10:11.585 --> 00:10:11.922
Kate Wood: I mean,

00:10:13.269 --> 00:10:17.292
what is interesting and what we know to be true about negative reviews is people are

00:10:17.292 --> 00:10:22.374
much more likely to give negative reviews in a digital society than they are positive reviews,

00:10:22.422 --> 00:10:26.493
right? We all have that kind of really tough experience and we just, we need to

00:10:26.493 --> 00:10:29.539
be able to like dislodge that emotion so we go write a negative review.

00:10:29.844 --> 00:10:31.575
That may not actually be how your business works.

00:10:31.623 --> 00:10:35.294
In the B2B space, it might be much more relational than it is kind of that

00:10:35.294 --> 00:10:41.081
tactical transaction. But I think if you can develop a relationship with someone such that they

00:10:41.081 --> 00:10:45.570
are dissatisfied with your service or your product, but you can understand why and you can

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:48.103
get to the heart of it and you can see and hear them.

00:10:48.215 --> 00:10:51.036
Most of us just want to be seen and heard from a psychology standpoint.

00:10:51.565 --> 00:10:58.507
You will get so much information about where within the business, across sales, product, customer success,

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:05.528
engineering, where you can start to deploy um the resources that you need to be able

00:11:05.528 --> 00:11:07.019
to start turning that narrative around.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:07.964
Wow.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:15.719
Casey Cheshire: So, someone listening to this who is like, okay, I have maybe over-indexed a little too

00:11:15.719 --> 00:11:18.828
much on the the quantitative side.

00:11:19.309 --> 00:11:24.083
I have shunned emotions, you know, like me, throw me in front of a good movie,

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:27.849
I may or may not get some blurry eyes from the pollen.

00:11:28.506 --> 00:11:31.374
But uh, yeah, how do we how do we follow the emotion?

00:11:31.566 --> 00:11:36.022
Have someone, you know, another marketing leader listening to this, if they're like, okay, I wanna

00:11:36.038 --> 00:11:38.221
dig in on the emotion. Maybe I think I'm doing it, maybe not.

00:11:38.301 --> 00:11:40.516
Like how how do they start leaning into that?

00:11:41.495 --> 00:11:43.790
Kate Wood: I would say kind of twofold storytelling and branding.

00:11:44.223 --> 00:11:48.653
Um so branding is really your identity, your personality, who you show up in as the

00:11:48.653 --> 00:11:51.927
world, as a company. And I think it's really important to know the science behind this.

00:11:52.409 --> 00:11:58.331
The human brain processes colors first, then shapes, then numbers, and then words.

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:02.231
Again, really important. Colors first matter.

00:12:02.376 --> 00:12:08.067
Your color palette, how you show up, The emotion you invoke through that, whether it's your

00:12:08.067 --> 00:12:13.563
typography, your photography, the angles that you use, the shapes that you leverage, all of these

00:12:13.916 --> 00:12:20.357
very sort of line-item elements bubble up into an overall brand identity that makes you feel

00:12:20.421 --> 00:12:22.280
something before you even know something.

00:12:23.113 --> 00:12:25.660
And then the knowing is where the storytelling comes in.

00:12:26.478 --> 00:12:31.589
Again, where have you made your clients look amazing to their internal stakeholders?

00:12:32.078 --> 00:12:34.906
Where do you know as a brand that you have room to improve?

00:12:35.386 --> 00:12:38.975
Most clients don't expect companies and vendors to be perfect.

00:12:39.296 --> 00:12:42.676
They do want them to be authentic and they do want progress.

00:12:43.061 --> 00:12:47.995
And so being honest and authentic about where your product strategy is going to make your

00:12:48.043 --> 00:12:52.834
business stronger, um the investments that you're making across the board from a process standpoint to

00:12:52.834 --> 00:12:56.311
create more connective tissue, to be able to get that feedback loop, to be able to

00:12:56.375 --> 00:13:01.037
create a better customer experience. Um I think the the kind of uh two sides of

00:13:01.566 --> 00:13:06.039
or the two sides of the brand identity and the storytelling coin go a really long

00:13:06.151 --> 00:13:06.392
way

00:13:08.059 --> 00:13:09.726
in terms of making that happen.

00:13:10.704 --> 00:13:10.816
Casey Cheshire: Hmm.

00:13:13.686 --> 00:13:18.416
The colors matter. Have you have you rebranded any colors for companies you've started working with?

00:13:19.041 --> 00:13:23.209
Kate Wood: Yeah, I think um I want to say 2 out of the last 4 companies I've

00:13:23.225 --> 00:13:26.239
been with, we have gone through several rebrands.

00:13:26.897 --> 00:13:31.642
Um and it's incredibly important. I often tell marketers to go to an industry event, Go

00:13:31.642 --> 00:13:34.190
to an industry event, look around at the vendors at the booths.

00:13:34.703 --> 00:13:38.356
It's almost like a carnival or like a circus, like when you're a little kid and

00:13:38.356 --> 00:13:40.551
you go and you're touching things and you're picking things up.

00:13:40.616 --> 00:13:43.356
Just watch the way your clients interact with your swag.

00:13:43.804 --> 00:13:47.362
Half the time they're thinking of their children, like, will this swag be good for my

00:13:47.426 --> 00:13:52.361
kids? That is actually an emotional experience that you can capitalize on and double down on.

00:13:52.698 --> 00:13:55.422
Just because someone wants a piece of swag for their kid doesn't mean that they don't

00:13:55.422 --> 00:13:57.537
want to talk to you about the piece of software that you're selling them.

00:13:58.087 --> 00:13:59.850
They're both and, as we say.

00:14:00.155 --> 00:14:05.985
And so I think that is kind of maybe one of the most tangible experiences I

00:14:06.033 --> 00:14:09.535
can call forth to say, this is why brand identity matters.

00:14:09.968 --> 00:14:11.542
This is why your color matters.

00:14:12.153 --> 00:14:18.063
The way you show up is how you make someone feel, not only about themselves, but

00:14:18.143 --> 00:14:21.677
also because of how you are making them feel by interacting with you.

00:14:21.869 --> 00:14:23.877
It's a little bit like The Company You Keep.

00:14:25.209 --> 00:14:27.456
Casey Cheshire: Yeah, company you keep. Interesting. You know what?

00:14:27.937 --> 00:14:30.312
This is a new a new view of this for me.

00:14:30.986 --> 00:14:38.929
Um, I usually get like to judge, laugh, scoff at the marketers who put too good

00:14:38.929 --> 00:14:40.277
of a prize in their booth.

00:14:41.015 --> 00:14:45.236
Um, and you know what? You mentioned the children, I'm like thinking 100% there.

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:50.306
And usually it's like, oh, this stuffed unicorn would be perfect for my daughter.

00:14:50.355 --> 00:14:53.163
She would love this. I don't even care what your thing is.

00:14:53.243 --> 00:14:56.499
I'm like, scan my badge, Give me the damn unicorn, let's go.

00:14:57.141 --> 00:15:02.369
And and I know that I'm gonna unsubscribe, but I think in that moment, there's an

00:15:02.369 --> 00:15:07.244
opportunity that maybe sales is thinking like I was thinking, which is like, yeah, we get

00:15:07.244 --> 00:15:08.799
it. Nobody wants to talk to me.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:13.642
Just take the stupid unicorn, and I know you're gonna be an unsubscribe, marketing stinks.

00:15:13.738 --> 00:15:17.074
But in that moment, what if they had talked to me about that?

00:15:17.667 --> 00:15:22.591
I would equate being a good dad and that vibe with their brand.

00:15:24.018 --> 00:15:27.658
Kate Wood: Yes, exactly. And also, I think this is important to note, to say as a marketer,

00:15:28.027 --> 00:15:31.203
you are not superhero, you're not everyone to everything.

00:15:31.556 --> 00:15:36.527
That is where having an insanely strong sales team is incredibly important because your job as

00:15:36.527 --> 00:15:39.622
a marketer is to get someone to the booth.

00:15:40.152 --> 00:15:44.386
Sales team's job is to do something with that attention once it's at the booth.

00:15:44.851 --> 00:15:48.026
And so as a marketer, if you can create an experience such that people want to

00:15:48.154 --> 00:15:52.068
lean in and come up to your booth, then as the sales team, It's their job

00:15:52.068 --> 00:15:55.761
to say to that dad who's come up and talked about their daughter and how they

